## 5. Self Projects (0-25 points)

### 5.1 Complexity Table

| Simple (low impact) | Complex (high impact) |
|---|---|
| Todo apps, calculators, basic CRUD apps, weather apps (public API), note-taking apps, recipe apps, simple portfolio sites, "Hello World" / classroom exercises, basic sentiment analysis with standard libraries (NLTK, scikit-learn), simple e-commerce or social-media clones | Multi-feature full-stack apps, apps with real user authentication *and* a real database, ML/AI applications, real-time apps (chat, streaming), mobile apps with native features, microservices architecture, apps with genuine user adoption, apps solving a real-world problem, apps demonstrating advanced algorithms/data structures |

**Basic CRUD apps — one rule:** score 0-3. Zero is reserved for a CRUD app with
no distinguishing feature at all; 1-3 is available only with a genuine added
feature (real auth, a non-trivial data model, etc).

### 5.2 Scoring Bands (before link adjustment)

**HIGH (18-25):** complex projects (per 5.1) with real-world impact, advanced
architecture, multiple technologies, genuine user adoption, or open-source
contribution woven into the project

**MEDIUM (9-17):** projects with some complexity, good documentation, multiple
features, or a moderate technical challenge

**LOW (1-8):** simple/tutorial projects per 5.1, classroom assignments, minimal
technical complexity (basic CRUD apps: see the 0-3 rule above, which sits inside
this band, not on top of it)

**ZERO (0):** no projects, or only projects demonstrating no technical skill at
all

### 5.3 Link Adjustment — the only mechanism that affects this score for link status

After assigning a band per 5.2, adjust down:
- **One sub-tier** (upper half of the band → lower half of the same band, or the
  bottom of a band → the top of the next band down) if the project has no
  verifiable link (no GitHub, live demo, or active URL)
- **Half a sub-tier** if the link is broken or inactive

No other percentage reduction or flat-point deduction applies to link status
anywhere else in this prompt.

### 5.4 Track Carve-Out

For `formal_it` / `related_field` candidates who are current full-time students,
a portfolio consisting mainly of coursework projects is expected and must not,
by itself, push the score below what 5.1-5.3 would otherwise produce. This
carve-out does not apply to `self_taught` candidates, for whom project history is
a primary signal.

